NECKLACE_DECK = "The season's fullest necklace works when the objects are personal, the chain is simple and one decision holds the apparent chaos together."
NECKLACE_BODY = f'''<div class="article-layout"><aside class="article-aside"><p class="kicker">The assembly guide</p><ol><li><a href="#answer">The short answer</a></li><li><a href="#why">Why it is rising</a></li><li><a href="#edit">The editing rule</a></li><li><a href="#build">Build the necklace</a></li><li><a href="#outfits">Six outfit formulas</a></li><li><a href="#problems">Fix common problems</a></li><li><a href="#care">Weight and care</a></li></ol><button class="copy-link" data-copy-link type="button">Copy link</button></aside><div class="article-body">
<p class="article-opening">A kitchen-sink necklace is a long, deliberately crowded necklace carrying an eclectic mix of charms, medallions, shells, letters, found objects and small keepsakes. To make it look intentional, use one plain chain, choose one dominant metal or colour and leave a little space between objects. Wear it over a quiet neckline and let it be the only complicated part of the outfit.</p>
<p>The name suggests indiscriminate accumulation. The good version is more precise. A brass saint medal can sit beside a silver key, a shell and an enamel fruit, but the eye still needs a route through them. Scale, spacing or colour must repeat somewhere. Without that rhythm, the necklace reads as a drawer emptied onto a cord. With it, unrelated objects begin to look like a private archive.</p>
<p>That tension is exactly why the idea feels current. Vogue identified the &ldquo;everything and the kitchen sink necklace&rdquo; in its fall 2026 runway report, citing the DIY spirit of charm-heavy pieces at Celine and Marni. Its September accessories update returned to long bohemian necklaces at Altuzarra, Celine, Marni and Dries Van Noten. The trend is maximal, but its real subject is personal selection rather than generic excess.</p>
<div class="summary-box" id="answer"><p class="kicker">The concise answer</p><h2>Choose one anchor, one rhythm and one quiet background</h2><p>Start with a 60-to-80-centimetre chain and one anchor charm roughly the size of a large coin. Add three to seven smaller objects that repeat either its metal, colour or shape. Keep the heaviest pieces near the centre, test the necklace while walking and remove anything that turns rather than hangs. Wear it over a crew neck, button-down, plain knit or simple dress with no competing earrings.</p></div>
<section id="why"><h2>Why the kitchen-sink necklace feels new now</h2><p>Charm necklaces are not new, and that is part of their appeal. A bracelet charm from childhood, a travel token and a pendant bought last week can occupy the same chain without pretending to share an origin. Fall 2026 styling makes that accumulation visible instead of hiding it inside a jewellery box.</p><p>The runway version is longer and less polished than the neat charm chains of recent seasons. Vogue's September report describes medallions, letters, shells and horns piled over knits and button-downs. The length matters. Once the objects fall below the collarbone, the necklace behaves more like a narrow vertical layer than a conventional piece of jewellery.</p><p>It also extends a larger shift already visible across by.foro's accessories coverage: the return of pieces that can be rearranged and interpreted. A <a href="/blogs/fashion/mahjong-necklace-meaning/">mahjong pendant</a> carries meaning in one object; <a href="/blogs/fashion/bandana-jewellery/">bandana jewellery</a> changes the chain itself; a kitchen-sink necklace turns the full collection into the message.</p><h3>Personal does not have to mean precious</h3><p>An object earns its place through shape, memory or visual use, not market value. A button can be more convincing than a gemstone if it repeats the warm brown of the coat beneath it. A small hotel key tag may matter more than a newly purchased &ldquo;vintage-inspired&rdquo; charm. The necklace looks strongest when at least one item could not have been chosen by an algorithm.</p></section>
{picture(NECKLACE_ROOT, "kitchen-sink-necklace-charms", "Vintage medallions, a small key, shell, enamel fruit and mismatched metal charms being arranged beside a long dark chain on worn oak")}
<section id="edit"><h2>The editing rule: repeat one thing, vary the rest</h2><p>A crowded necklace needs a quiet form of repetition. Choose one variable to control and allow the others to move. If most pieces are mixed metals, repeat rounded shapes. If shapes range from keys to shells and letters, keep the palette largely brass. If colour is the attraction, use a simple dark cord and similar spacing.</p><div class="article-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Kitchen-sink necklace editing rules" tabindex="0"><table class="article-visual-table"><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Let vary</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mostly one metal</td><td>Shape, age and subject</td><td>Rich but coherent</td></tr><tr><td>Repeated round shapes</td><td>Metal and colour</td><td>Eclectic with rhythm</td></tr><tr><td>One colour family</td><td>Material and scale</td><td>Playful, not random</td></tr><tr><td>Even spacing</td><td>Almost everything else</td><td>Graphic and readable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not try to control every variable. Matching the metal, shape, colour, age and finish turns the necklace back into a coordinated set. The interest comes from difference; the editing rule simply prevents that difference from becoming visual noise.</p><h3>Choose an anchor before the supporting cast</h3><p>The anchor is the piece the eye finds first. It may be the largest medallion, the palest shell or the only coloured charm. Place it at or just below the centre, then build asymmetrically around it. Perfect symmetry can make found objects look mass-produced. A one-charm difference between the two sides usually feels more natural.</p></section>
<section id="build"><h2>How to build one from jewellery you already own</h2><ol><li><strong>Lay out every candidate.</strong> Include pendants, single earrings, keys, ribbons and small brooches with secure loops, but exclude anything sharp, fragile or impossible to clean.</li><li><strong>Choose the chain by weight.</strong> A fine chain can hold two light charms; a full arrangement needs a stronger curb, belcher or cable link. Check the clasp as carefully as the links.</li><li><strong>Set the anchor.</strong> Put the largest or most meaningful object near the centre and test where it falls against a plain top.</li><li><strong>Add in odd numbers.</strong> Three, five or seven supporting objects are easier to stagger than a matched pair on either side.</li><li><strong>Create breathing room.</strong> Leave enough link between objects for each silhouette to remain visible. Crowded does not mean fused.</li><li><strong>Perform the movement test.</strong> Walk, sit and bend. Remove the charm that flips, strikes another loudly or pulls the whole chain sideways.</li></ol><p>Use removable jump rings or small locking connectors if you want the arrangement to change. Permanent soldering is safer for valuable pieces but removes the playful, evolving quality of the trend. Ask a jeweller to assess antique or sentimental objects before altering them.</p><h3>Know what the symbols say</h3><p>Not every found object is neutral. Religious medals, military insignia, cultural symbols and game pieces may carry meanings beyond their graphic appeal. Research an unfamiliar object before wearing it. The same principle matters with <a href="/blogs/fashion/mahjong-necklace-meaning/">mahjong jewellery</a>: understanding the symbol makes the styling more personal, not less.</p></section>
<section id="outfits"><h2>Six outfits that give the necklace enough room</h2><h3>1. Grey crew-neck knit and dark trousers</h3><p>Let the necklace sit entirely on the knit rather than crossing the neckline. Grey accommodates silver, brass and colour without making any one metal look accidental. Keep the shoe simple and the bag hardware quiet.</p><h3>2. White shirt, buttoned high</h3><p>Wear a long chain over a crisp shirt, with the collar acting as a frame. The contrast between orderly cloth and irregular objects is the point. Skip a scarf, brooch and statement cuff.</p><h3>3. Black column dress</h3><p>A plain knitted or jersey dress turns the necklace into a vertical line. Choose a length that ends above the waist seam, or remove the belt so the two horizontals do not compete.</p><h3>4. Denim shirt and tailored coat</h3><p>Blue denim prevents antique brass from looking ceremonial. Keep the necklace inside the open coat so its edges create a second frame. This formula also works with the restrained approach in our <a href="/blogs/fashion/how-to-wear-turquoise-accessories/">turquoise accessories guide</a>.</p><h3>5. Soft blouse with a bare neckline</h3><p>Choose fewer, lighter charms and let the chain touch skin. Avoid lace, ruching or a bow at the same height. The blouse can be romantic; its neckline must remain visually clear.</p><h3>6. T-shirt and blazer</h3><p>This is the lowest-risk formula. A washed T-shirt absorbs the DIY quality while a tailored jacket supplies structure. If the lapel still needs something, use a tiny quiet pin rather than the <a href="/blogs/fashion/textured-brooch-trend/">oversized textured brooch</a> you would wear on another day.</p></section>
{picture(NECKLACE_ROOT, "kitchen-sink-necklace-outfit", "Long mixed-charm necklace worn over a plain charcoal crew-neck knit beneath an open camel coat, cropped from chin to hip on a real city pavement")}
<section id="problems"><h2>Why a crowded necklace sometimes looks wrong</h2><h3>Every charm is trying to be the anchor</h3><p>If four objects are equally large, bright and detailed, the eye cannot decide where to rest. Keep one, move one to another chain and replace the others with flatter supporting pieces. The discarded charms are not failures; they may become the beginning of a second necklace.</p><h3>The chain disappears</h3><p>A chain that is too fine makes heavy objects seem suspended and anxious. Choose links visible enough to belong to the composition. Conversely, an enormous chain plus many charms can look industrial. One element should supply the mass; the other should carry it.</p><h3>The necklace fights the neckline</h3><p>Do not let the anchor repeatedly cross a V-neck, lapel edge or button placket. Shorten or lengthen the chain until the pendant lands clearly on skin or cloth. A five-centimetre change often fixes an outfit more effectively than replacing any charm.</p><h3>Everything looks newly bought</h3><p>Uniform patina, identical jump rings and a ready-made story can make eclectic jewellery feel strangely impersonal. Mix in one piece you already own and allow modest wear to remain. Do not artificially damage new metal or misrepresent reproduction objects as antique.</p></section>
<section id="care"><h2>Weight, skin and garment care matter</h2><p>Weigh the complete necklace if it begins to pull. There is no universal comfortable number: chain thickness, object distribution, neckline and individual sensitivity all matter. The useful test is whether you forget the piece for ten minutes. Persistent pressure at the back of the neck, red skin or a clasp rotating forward means the arrangement needs fewer objects or a different chain.</p><p>Mixed metals may react differently to moisture and cleaning products. Wipe pieces individually with a soft dry cloth, keep plated and porous objects away from aggressive dips and do not soak shells, glued stones, pearls, painted enamel or paper inclusions. Store the necklace flat with its charms separated so chains do not knot and hard edges do not scratch softer surfaces.</p><p>Before converting a brooch or antique pendant, inspect every loop. A connector is only as secure as the thinnest old ring attached to it. For valuable or irreplaceable pieces, let a professional jeweller add a reversible bail.</p></section>
<section class="article-faq"><h2>Kitchen-sink necklace questions</h2><h3>What is a kitchen-sink necklace?</h3><p>It is a long, eclectic necklace carrying multiple charms, medallions and small objects. The fall 2026 version is deliberately abundant and often mixes shapes, eras and materials.</p><h3>How many charms should it have?</h3><p>Begin with one anchor and three supporting charms. Add up to seven if the chain remains balanced and each object is still visible. More is possible, but weight and noise become practical limits.</p><h3>Can you mix gold and silver charms?</h3><p>Yes. Repeat at least one of the metals twice and keep another element consistent, such as rounded shapes or even spacing. One isolated silver charm on an otherwise brass necklace can look accidental.</p><h3>Can it be worn with other jewellery?</h3><p>Use small earrings and one quiet ring or bracelet. Avoid another statement necklace and keep the lapel free. The crowded necklace needs to remain the outfit's only complicated sentence.</p></section>
<section class="source-note"><h2>Sources and image note</h2><p>Runway context was reviewed in Vogue's <a href="https://www.vogue.com/article/the-best-fall-2026-accessory-trends">March 2026 fall accessory report</a> and its <a href="https://www.vogue.com/article/fall-2026-accessory-trends">4 September fall accessories update</a>. Styling, assembly, symbolism and care guidance is by.foro editorial analysis. Antique and sentimental pieces should be assessed by a qualified jeweller before alteration. The photographs show fictional, unbranded jewellery scenes created for this article.</p></section>
<section><h2>The best version should keep changing</h2><p>A kitchen-sink necklace is not finished when the clasp closes. Remove the charm that annoys you, add the object that acquires a story and change the chain when the weight changes. Its coherence comes from one person's editing over time. That is why the necklace can be full without feeling generic.</p></section><div class="article-end"><span>End</span><p>Published by by.foro Editorial on 5 September 2026. For corrections, contact <a href="mailto:hello@byforo.com">hello@byforo.com</a>.</p></div></div></div>'''


